      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to color a hexagon and its nearby neighbors such that no two adjacent hexagons share the same color. This is a classic problem of graph coloring where we are essentially trying to color the vertices of a graph with three colors such that no two adjacent vertices share the same color.\n\nThe given hexagon and its neighbors form a graph with the centers of the hexagons at the vertices and edges as the connections. The graph can be visualized as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}\n\\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (B) at (10,0);\n\\coordinate (C) at (15,8.7);\n\\coordinate (D) at (10,17.3);\n\\coordinate (E) at (0,17.3);\n\\coordinate (F) at (-5,8.7);\n\\coordinate (G) at (0,0);\n\n\\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (E) -- (F) -- (G);\n\\draw (A) -- (C) -- (D);\n\\draw (A) -- (E) -- (B);\n\\draw (B) -- (F) -- (G);\n\\draw (C) -- (F) -- (G);\n\\draw (D) -- (E) -- (F);\n\\draw (E) -- (F) -- (G);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the graph is composed of a central hexagon with four outer hexagons surrounding it. Each outer hexagon is connected to the central hexagon and to two of the other outer hexagons.\n\nThe key observation is that the central hexagon (R) must be a different color from all four outer hexagons. Each of the outer hexagons can be colored in one of three ways, but they must be different from each other and from the central hexagon.\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. The central hexagon (R) can be colored in 3 ways.\n2. Each of the four outer hexagons can be colored in 2 ways (since it cannot be the same color as the central hexagon and cannot be the same color as each other).\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to color the figure is:\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^4 = 3 \\times 16 = 48 \\]\n\nSo, the number of different ways to color the figure is \\(\\boxed{48}\\).",
